-# POST requests/uploads
-
-Can either upload a file in
-- body of the request
-or
-- as part of a multipart
-
-## Multipart
-
-Each multipart-part can potentially contain a file
-Normally the rule is that 
-
-Parsing sequence is as follows (when the 'Content-Type' header is 'multipart/form-data'):
-
-- check for boundary : if not found at start of body, return error (as body is invalid)
-- parse headers
-    - must have 'Content-Disposition: form-data' (i.e. value=form-data)
-        - must have 'name' parameter
-        - if name=file, 'filename' parameter must be present
-        - filename must be 
-        -      sanitised
-        -      decoded if UTF-8
-
-    - may have Content-Type
-- validate headers
-- parse body
-- validate body
-
-part.isFile is set to true whenever a file is to be uploaded
-
-### Content-Disposition 
-
-Value : must be 'form-data'
-
-Parameters : 
-- 'name' : must be 'file'
-if 'name=file'
-- 'filename' : must be present and follow filename rules
--            : must not already exist
-             : normalised/sanitised/decoded
-
-### File handling/parsing
-
-If a filename is provided, and the method is POST, then the following steps are taken to handle the file upload :
-
-Body is read into tempfile (boundaries and headers are not, but these are included in content-length calculations (only those appearing in body!))
-If no errors, and the file does not already exist, the tempfile is renamed using the upload path and filename 
-<!-- (We probably need to have separate folders for each server?) -->
-
-- tempfile      : created during parsing
-                : each time buffer is read, the new buffer is appended to tempfile
-                : body is read until content-length has been reached
-                : name is stored in the multipart-part as 'part.tempfile'
-
-- upload_base   : created in 'Request::_re.paths.upload_base' 
-                : created when matching server/location (after parsing headers, and before parsing body)
-   
-- filename      :  
-                : filename from content-disposition will be normalised and appended to base upload path (at time of mp_header validation)
-                : this will be a fileInfo_s which contains info on file type/permissions/accessibility
-                : if 'fileInfo_s.not_found' == 'false', then the file already exists : return error (or overwritten?)
-                : magic number compared with filetype (taken from ending) : if not matching, return error
-                : The only HTTP limitations on filename are that they must follow header-field parameter rules : 
-                    
-                    So the filename is allowed to consist of : 
-                                            
-                                token = 1*tchar
-                            or
-                                quoted-string = DQUOTE *( qdtext / quoted-pair ) DQUOTE
-
-- filename*   : if filename* is present, then this indicates an encoded filename
-
-                e.g.       : filename*=UTF-8''%E2%82%ACrates.pdf
-                
-                format     : charset'language'percent-encoded-value
-                            
-                            charset                 : 
-                                                        UTF-8
-                                                        ISO-8859-1
-                                                        ascii
-
-                            language                : ignored by us
-                            percent-encoded-value   : must be valid hex range ^%[0-9A-Fa-f]{1}[0-9A-Fa-f]{1}
-
-#### FILENAME VALIDATION
-
-HTTP does not define:
-
-    Allowed filesystem characters (/ \ : * ? etc.)
-    Maximum filename length
-    Path vs filename (e.g. ../../etc/passwd)
-    OS-specific restrictions
-
-👉 These are application/security concerns, not protocol rules.
-
-When validating filenames from HTTP:
-You should:
--Strip directory paths (../, /, \)
--Normalize encoding
--Reject control characters
--Enforce your own whitelist (e.g. [a-zA-Z0-9._-])
--Limit length
\ No newline at end of file
